Definition: jQuery is a fast, lightweight, cross-platform JavaScript library designed to simplify HTML DOM manipulation, event handling, animation, and AJAX interactions.

Key Features:

DOM traversal and manipulation with simple selectors ($("#id"), $(".class")).

Event handling (.click(), .on()).

AJAX support ($.ajax(), $.get(), $.post()).

Effects and animations (.hide(), .fadeIn(), .slideToggle()).

Cross-browser compatibility.

Advantages:

Reduces boilerplate JavaScript code.

Provides chainable methods.

Large plugin ecosystem.

Limitations:

Adds extra dependency (not needed in modern JS frameworks).

Vanilla JS and frameworks (React, Angular, Vue) often replace it.

❓ 25 Deep-Dive Q&A on jQuery
Basics
Q: What is jQuery and why was it created?  
A: jQuery is a JavaScript library created to simplify DOM manipulation, event handling, and AJAX. It abstracts browser inconsistencies and provides a concise API.

Q: How do you include jQuery in a project?  
A: Either via CDN (<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>) or by downloading and hosting locally.

Q: What is the difference between $(document).ready() and window.onload?  
A: $(document).ready() executes when the DOM is fully loaded, while window.onload waits for all resources (images, scripts) to load.

Q: Explain the $ symbol in jQuery.  
A: $ is just an alias for the jQuery function. It’s used to select elements and apply methods.

Q: What is chaining in jQuery?  
A: Chaining allows multiple methods to be called on the same selection in one line, e.g., $("#box").css("color","red").slideUp().slideDown();.

Selectors & DOM
Q: How does jQuery select elements differently from JavaScript?  
A: jQuery uses CSS-like selectors ($(".class"), $("#id")) instead of verbose JS methods like document.getElementById.

Q: Difference between .find() and .children()?  
A: .find() searches all descendants, .children() only searches direct children.

Q: How do you get or set text and HTML content?  
A: .text() for plain text, .html() for HTML markup.

Q: How do you add/remove classes dynamically?  
A: .addClass("className"), .removeClass("className"), .toggleClass("className").

Q: How do you manipulate attributes in jQuery?  
A: .attr("attributeName") to get, .attr("attributeName","value") to set.

Events
Q: How do you bind events in jQuery?  
A: Using .on("event", selector, handler) or shorthand methods like .click(handler).

Q: Difference between .bind(), .live(), .delegate(), and .on()?  
A: .on() is the modern unified method. .bind() attaches directly, .live() was deprecated, .delegate() attaches to parent for dynamic elements.

Q: How do you prevent default behavior in jQuery?  
A: Use event.preventDefault() inside the handler.

Q: How do you stop event bubbling?  
A: Use event.stopPropagation().

Q: How do you trigger an event manually?  
A: .trigger("eventName").

AJAX
Q: How do you make an AJAX call in jQuery?  
A: Using $.ajax({url:"/api", type:"GET", success:function(data){}});.

Q: Difference between $.ajax(), $.get(), and $.post()?  
A: $.ajax() is the most flexible, $.get() and $.post() are shorthand for GET and POST requests.

Q: How do you handle JSON data in jQuery AJAX?  
A: Set dataType:"json" in $.ajax() and access parsed JSON in the success callback.

Q: How do you handle AJAX errors?  
A: Use the error callback in $.ajax() or .fail() with promises.

Q: How do you load content into a div using jQuery?  
A: $("#div").load("file.html");.

Effects & Plugins
Q: How do you hide/show elements in jQuery?  
A: .hide(), .show(), .toggle().

Q: Difference between .fadeIn() and .slideDown()?  
A: .fadeIn() changes opacity, .slideDown() changes height.

Q: How do you animate CSS properties?  
A: .animate({width:"200px",opacity:0.5},1000);.

Q: What are jQuery plugins?  
A: Reusable code modules built on jQuery to extend functionality (e.g., sliders, modals).

Q: How do you create a custom plugin in jQuery?  
A: Extend $.fn with a function:

js
$.fn.highlight = function() {
  this.css("background","yellow");
  return this;
